// A `?.` call allocates twice in its present branch: the value, and the box
// holding the optional. Both used to leak, 8 bytes per iteration.
//
// The box is claimed on the *merged* value rather than where it is malloc'd.
// Recording it inside the branch was the original blocker: the scope's release
// runs after the branches rejoin, so a release recorded in the branch does not
// dominate it, and the emitted IR was invalid.
//
// The box's plan is `Deep`, not the flat `Value` it started as: every
// string-returning method reachable through `?.` genuinely allocates, so a box
// this becomes the *sole* owner of, bound to a name, no `??` at all, has to
// recurse into its payload when released, or that payload leaks forever.
// `Deep` on a `Type::Optional` already does exactly that, null-checked, via
// `emit_async_drop_value_in_place`'s own `Type::Optional` arm, the same glue a
// plain scope-owned optional local already used.
//
// The `??` case is the one place that would be wrong: it hands the payload out
// *by value*, cloned, so the merged result is independently owned, recursing
// into the original there too would free memory the result still needs.
// `emit_null_coalesce` accounts for this itself: it forgets this exact
// registration and frees only the box's own header, bypassing the plan
// entirely, once it has dealt with the payload on its own terms (cloning it,
// and, for the one shape provable, `left` exactly an `OptionalMethodCall`,
// discarding the original after the clone; `OptionalFieldAccess` does not get
// this, since a struct field can hold a static string a literal was built
// with, and freeing that unconditionally segfaulted before this was narrowed).
//
// A second, separate bug surfaced alongside: releasing the coalesce's own
// merged result relies on `emit_async_cleanup_source_expression`, which only
// frees a value when it recognizes the *shape* of the expression that produced
// it. `BinOp::NullCoalesce` was not a shape it recognized, so a clone was
// silently never freed, forgetting a pending registration only stops it being
// tracked, it does not itself call `free`. Now recognized, mirroring the exact
// condition the coalesce itself uses to decide whether its result is owned.
//
// A third: `OptionalFieldAccess` had never registered its own box's release at
// all, unlike `OptionalMethodCall`, an 8-byte-per-iteration leak neither a
// bound local nor a `??` could ever have claimed, found while wiring the two
// fixes above. `Value`, not `Deep`, for the same static-field reason.
//
// The last, non-scaling residue, `const label: string? = "abc";`, boxed by
// the shared `emit_optional_box` helper, which never registered its own
// release, closed differently from the other three: not inside
// `emit_optional_box`/`emit_expr_as` themselves (two attempts there each broke
// a different IR snapshot test the same way the very first box-release attempt
// in this file did, some callers, a numeric range check and a `&&=`/`||=`/
// `??=` short-circuit assignment, use the box *inside* a branch a caller later
// merges into a phi, and a release recorded pre-merge does not dominate a
// later use of it), but at the one place proven safe regardless of what
// branching happened *inside* `emit_expr_as`: the binding, right after it
// returns a value. A callee's return always dominates its own call site, by
// construction, so registering there needs no knowledge of the branches that
// produced it. Narrowed to values whose own type is not already `Optional`
// (excludes `OptionalMethodCall`/`OptionalFieldAccess`, which register
// themselves, and a plain read of an existing binding, which is a borrow) and
// to nothing else pending at that point, boxing an expression that itself
// allocates and separately registers, `"a".toUpperCase()` coerced into
// `string?`, hit a genuinely separate, pre-existing double-free in
// `emit_async_cleanup_source_expression`'s own `Optional` handling (it frees an
// unwrapped payload by matching the *expression's shape* rather than by
// forgetting the original registration's SSA identity, so both fire).
// Confirmed unrelated to anything above, it reproduces identically with this
// fix's condition forced false, and left alone rather than folded in here.
//
// The shapes below exist because every fix above is only safe if it holds for
// all of them: a temporary, a bound coalesce result, a bound-but-unconsumed
// optional, an absent receiver taking the other branch, and a bare literal
// binding with no `?.` in sight. Expected output is 1100.
const absentLabel = (): string? => {
    return;
};

const main = (): void => {
    const label: string? = "abc";
    const missing: string? = absentLabel();
    let total: usize = 0;
    for (let i: i32 = 0; i < 100; i += 1) {
        total += (label?.toUpperCase() ?? "x").length;

        const bound: string = label?.toUpperCase() ?? "x";
        total += bound.length;

        const optional: string? = label?.toUpperCase();
        total += optional?.length ?? 0;

        total += (missing?.toUpperCase() ?? "yz").length;

        const absent: string? = missing?.toUpperCase();
        total += absent?.length ?? 0;
    }
    console.log(total);
    console.log("assert:", 1100);
};
